WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.480
A judge froze wall construction across 500 miles of the Texas, Mexico border. This is Omni

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.600
View North America, episode 10. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:17.120
The order came Friday from Judge Kathleen Cardone in the U.S. District Court for the Western

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.420
District of Texas, Newsweek reports. Federal agencies shall not build a barrier,

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:28.360
related works, or disturb the ground in the big bend sector until they comply with all applicable

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.700
That sector is about a quarter of the U.S.-Mexico line, and it includes Big Bend National Park.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:40.180
The National Park Service says the park itself has 118 miles of border with Mexico.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:47.120
Cardone found a coalition of environmental groups and local landowners likely to succeed on at least one claim.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:50.680
She did not decide the underlying case for building the wall there.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:56.360
Across the region, more than 380,000 eligible voters in Surrey, British Columbia

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:01.660
face two elections one week apart, the Globe and Mail reports. Municipal and

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:05.440
Provincial Votes overlapped this month for the first time since the Province's

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:11.180
Election Act passed in 1995. On October 17th, they pick a mayor, eight

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:17.320
counselors, and six school trustees, then an MLA on October 24th. Nearly 100

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:21.120
municipal candidates are on the Surrey ballot, and at least 20 provincial

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.560
candidates across 10 writings. Premier David E.B. called that Provincial

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.620
vote on September 22nd after the local races had been set for years.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.300
Experts warned turnout could suffer, especially in the municipal contests.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:40.020
Two upstart right-wing parties say they will run in a majority of the 93 writings, after

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.200
the provincial conservatives split and partly came back together.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:49.360
Five members of the U.S. House have called out prices at 7-11 and Circle K. The Guardian found

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.340
those chains often charge more at the register or the pump than the shelf tag or the outdoor

00:01:54.340 --> 00:02:00.600
fuel sign. Becca Ballant, a Democrat from Vermont, said shoppers are stuck fact-checking every price,

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:05.160
and she asked the Federal Trade Commission to investigate if the companies are misleading people

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:10.680
on purpose. Nanette Baragon, a Democrat whose district covers part of Los Angeles County,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:15.860
called the findings disturbing and said President Trump and Republicans are lifting the cost of

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:23.380
food and basics. From 2023 to 2025, 7-11 failed 41 percent of price inspections in Arizona,

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:33.780
and 47% in Colorado, and in Utah, circle K failed 35% in Florida, 39% in Arizona, and 62% in North Carolina.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:41.100
The chains did not answer the Guardian's questions. President Trump has called affordability worries a hoax and a con job.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:47.580
An AP Nork poll out Thursday found 17% of Americans approve of how he handles the cost of living.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:53.300
Also, today, New York Times and Sienna Poles find Democrats performing strongly in five

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.980
reliably Republican states where they are trying to flip Senate seats.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:03.200
NPR says the Supreme Court term opens Monday, with cases on religion, guns, and climate,

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.620
and the emergency docket is still active.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:11.000
The Guardian reports a Brookings analysis that ranks Salinas, California, the least affordable

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.480
U.S. city for the middle class.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:17.240
The Washington Post says a record number of Native Americans are running in the midterms.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:23.220
In the wider world, the USS Abraham Lincoln is expected in San Diego this week after more

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.040
than 250 days at sea, the Guardian reports.

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:28.700
Relatives of the sailors say they are relieved.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:33.760
A mother there had a large, well-concined maid, and she said she cannot wait to see her son.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:38.920
The case on both sides is whether wall work in big bend can start again before those laws

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:39.260
are met.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:44.320
The landowners and environmental groups want it kept stopped, and Cardone said they face

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:46.480
serious, irreparable harm if it does not.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.500
She also said the balance of equities and the public interest favor them.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:55.840
President Trump has made cutting illegal crossings a main aim of his presidency, Newsweek says.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:02.580
His 2025 law set aside $46.5 billion for fencing and other obstacles along the

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:08.400
1,954-mile southern border. The plans include hundreds of miles of 30-foot wall,

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:12.840
plus cameras and river barriers on private land and on federal land.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:18.000
In August, bulldozers were clearing ground in the National Park, and that drew anger

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.000
from both parties.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.900
What both sides have to live with is a pause, not a final ruling on whether the wall

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:24.720
may be built there.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.340
Agency's stay barred from barriers, related works, and ground disturbance in the sector until

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:31.940
they meet all applicable laws.

